+### r_SceneReferredExportTap
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0 |
+| Purpose | **Which** frame `rec_CaptureEXR` writes. **0 = graded**: the value `HDRFinalPass` has immediately before the output transform - white balance, CDL, look LUT, bloom, sun shafts and vignette applied - handed back to linear ACEScg. This is the acceptance instrument: the only tap for which "apply the same ODT elsewhere and compare" tests the output transform instead of every operator in front of it. **1 = negative**: the HDR target as it enters the tone map, exposed and in ACEScg but ungraded, unvignetted and un-composited - the file to take into a colour suite. They are two different artefacts, not two settings of one. |
+
+### r_SceneReferredExportEncoding
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0 |
+| Purpose | What the exported EXR claims to be. **0 = ACES2065-1**: AP0 primaries, converted from AP1 during the write, tagged with AP0 `chromaticities` **and** `acesImageContainerFlag`. An untagged EXR in an ACES project is assumed to be ACES2065-1, so this file imports correctly with the operator doing nothing. **1 = ACEScg**: the buffer byte for byte, AP1 `chromaticities` and never the container flag - that flag asserts ST 2065-4 conformance, which is a claim about AP0 data. Costs a per-clip input transform in Resolve; right for a comp pipeline working in AP1. |
+
+### r_SceneReferredExportCompression
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 3 |
+| Purpose | EXR codec: 0 none, 1 RLE, 2 ZIPS (one scanline per block), 3 ZIP (16 scanlines - the CG default), 4 PIZ. ZIP roughly halves the disk rate for a cost that is already on a worker thread. None is for when the disk is fast and the CPU is not, and for reading a file with a hex editor. Lossless compression makes the file size content-dependent, so a frame that compresses badly is a frame the writer takes longer over. |
+
+### r_SceneReferredExportFixedStep
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Default | 0.0333 (30 fps) |
+| Purpose | The frame time a capturing **sequence** forces; `t_FixedStep` is saved on start and restored on stop. Outside the editor nothing else locks frame time - `capture_frames` does not, whatever its name suggests - so without this a sequence is one file per rendered frame at whatever rate the machine managed, and N frames is not N/fps seconds. An EXR sequence is a timed artefact or it cannot be conformed. 0 = leave `t_FixedStep` alone and accept that the result has no cadence. |
+
